.TH smi-pki-create-client-cert "1" "September 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI PKI Command"
.SH NAME
smi-pki-create-client-cert \- Create a complete client certificate on the CA side
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-pki-create-client-cert
.I DIRECTORY CLIENT_NAME ROOT_DOMAIN
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Runs \fBsmi-pki-do-client-cert-request\fR(1) and \fBsmi-pki-do-client-signing\fR(1) in one go, for a client that does not create its own key, such as an internal service.
Writes \fICLIENT_NAME\fB.client.priv.key\fR, \fB.client.pub.key\fR, \fB.client.csr\fR, \fB.client.crt\fR, \fB.client.ext\fR and \fB.client.chain.crt\fR in \fIDIRECTORY\fR.
The Intermediate CA of \fIROOT_DOMAIN\fR is checked first, so nothing is generated when it is missing.
.PP
The private key is created on the CA side and has to be delivered to the client securely; when the client can run \fBsmi-pki-do-client-cert-request\fR(1) itself, prefer that, so the key never leaves the client.
It is a thin command line wrapper over the matching function in the file based PKI library
\fBpki.sh\fR, so each step of building a CA and signing certificates can be run and inspected
on its own. All material is passed as file names; the command fails if the underlying OpenSSL
operation fails.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS
.nf
smi-pki-create-client-cert /var/opt/pki intranet-bot gintra
.fi
.RE
